Q: ASP.NET WebForms: page data not cleared after redirect when returning with browser back A: <p>Here's a few different options to handle this with JavaScript:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>You can detect if the page was navigated to from the browser history like in <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/45469414">this answer</a>.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Another option would be to show a panel (or if you have nothing to execute on the server side, just a button) with JavaScript telling it to redirect and remove the current page from navigation history: <code>window.location.replace('your-link');</code></p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>This code below will work for detecting if the page shown is loaded from cache instead of from the server, for instance if it was from forward/backwards navigation since that's what I needed in my application.</p>
<pre><code>document.getElementsByTagName(&quot;BODY&quot;)[0].onpageshow = function() {
    if (event.persisted) {
        alert('Please refresh');
        //your code here for what to do if page gets displayed from cache
    }
};
</code></pre>
<p>The <a href="https://www.w3schools.com/jsref/event_onpageshow.asp" rel="nofollow noreferrer">onpageshow</a> event happens whenever the page is displayed, so it works just fine with navigation. <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/PageTransitionEvent/persisted" rel="nofollow noreferrer">event.persisted</a> is a read-only property indicating if the webpage is loading from a cache. This ended up as the perfect solution for me when I was looking to do something similar.</p>
<h3>Why your code isn't clearing the fields</h3>
<p>When a method redirects, the only thing it tells the browser to do is redirect. It cannot pass the information required to tell your page to reset.</p>

Q: How can I add a script to documentation pages in a Docusaurus theme? A: <p>In Docusaurus v2 (and v3) you add JavaScript by specifying it in <code>docusaurus.config.js</code>/<code>docusaurus.config.mjs</code>. (Assuming the JavaScript must be present on every page.)</p>
<h2>Reference JavaScript File</h2>
<p>If you want to reference a JavaScript file, see <a href="https://docusaurus.io/docs/2.x/api/docusaurus-config#scripts" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>scripts</code></a>.</p>
<h3>Example</h3>
<p><strong><code>docusaurus.config.mjs</code></strong></p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>/** @type {import('@docusaurus/types').Config} */
const config = {
  scripts: [
    {
      src: 'https://plausible.example.org/js/script.js',
      defer: true,
      'data-domain': 'example.org' // You can optionally add custom attributes too!
    }
  ]
};

export default config;
</code></pre>
<p>As DeusProx rightly pointed out, if you want to insert your own JavaScript file, you can include it in the static directory and refer to it in this config.</p>
<h2>Inline JavaScript</h2>
<p>If you want execute inline JavaScript, see <a href="https://docusaurus.io/docs/2.x/api/docusaurus-config#headTags" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>headTags</code></a> instead.</p>
<h3>Example</h3>
<p><strong><code>docusaurus.config.mjs</code></strong></p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>/** @type {import('@docusaurus/types').Config} */
const config = {
  headTags: [
    {
      tagName: 'script',
      attributes: {}, // Mandatory, but no properties are required.
      innerHTML: 'window.plausible=window.plausible||function(){(plausible.q=plausible.q||[]).push(arguments)},plausible.init=plausible.init||function(i){plausible.o=i||{}};plausible.init()'
    }
  ]
};

export default config;
</code></pre>

Q: NLog EventProperties in .NET Core A: <p>Because the topic contains <strong>Properties</strong>, let me add 2 more example which may help future peoples fall here.</p>
<p>Example 1; adding static properties to be sent with every log, your target must support properties. With Seq target you can set static properties with a code like this:</p>
<pre><code>var seqTarget = new SeqTarget
{
    Name = &quot;seq-target&quot;,
    ServerUrl = &quot;http://my_seq_server_ip:5341&quot;,
    ApiKey = &quot;api_key_on_seq_for_AppName&quot;,
};

seqTarget.Properties.Add(new SeqPropertyItem
{
    Name = &quot;Application&quot;,
    Value = &quot;AppName_related_to_api_key&quot;
});

seqTarget.Properties.Add(new SeqPropertyItem
{
    Name = &quot;my_another_static_property&quot;,
    Value = Environment.MachineName
});
</code></pre>
<p>Example 2; If you want some extra property later when logging. If you want to log a property which has a long content for example, you will not want to show it on the log's header probably but in log details. Then use WithProperties method:</p>
<pre><code>MyHelper.logger.WithProperties(new List&lt;KeyValuePair&lt;string,object&gt;&gt; { 
new KeyValuePair&lt;string, object&gt;(&quot;UserName&quot;, userNameVariable),
new KeyValuePair&lt;string, object&gt;(&quot;UserPassword&quot;, passwordVariable),
new KeyValuePair&lt;string, object&gt;(&quot;LoginRequestJson&quot;,  jsonString)
}).Warn(&quot;{ProcessId} Unable to login.&quot;, &quot;LoginForm.Login&quot;);
</code></pre>
<p>See also: <a href="https://github.com/NLog/NLog/wiki/EventProperties-Layout-Renderer" rel="nofollow noreferrer">NLog EventProperties</a></p>
<p>See also: <a href="https://github.com/NLog/NLog.Extensions.Logging/wiki/NLog-properties-with-Microsoft-Extension-Logging" rel="nofollow noreferrer">NLog properties with Microsoft Extension Logging</a></p>
<p>Hope this helps.</p>
